Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Period live-action drama. Milwaukee, late 1990s. A cramped fifth-floor walk-up in a big brick building. THE NARRATOR, an unnamed man in his late twenties with a lean build, short brown hair, old jeans and a pizza-parlor shirt, watches from the window.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. I’m not talking about that stupid book where everybody could see everything you were doing. I’m talking about my real Big Brother.

NICK, early thirties and powerfully built, mounts the stairs carrying six grocery bags. His reddish-brown hair is uncombed, his face roughened by brawls, with scars on his chin and over his left eyebrow. Heavy boots and a brown suede jacket complete the uniform.

A burnt-orange 1980 Pontiac Trans Am with yellow flames and a hood eagle waits below.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. When he walked into a bar or a gym, people took notice, and man, did they move out of the way.

Nick deposits the bags beside MA, a stooped woman in her mid-sixties with graying brown hair, tired brown eyes and a faded cardigan.

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

THE NARRATOR
I don’t know. Why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She starts sleepin’ all day, pretty soon she won’t want to go outside or eat or do nothing.

THE NARRATOR
I’ll do better.

The Narrator’s community-college brochure peeks from beneath a pizza menu. Nick picks it up.

THE NARRATOR
Could you get me a job at the metal factory?

NICK
You don’t need a degree to get a good job. All you need is to know somebody.

THE NARRATOR
Then talk to your boss about me.

NICK
What kind of shit do you want to take at school anyway? You’re not gonna be a doctor or lawyer. Hell, you’re too old anyway.

Nick drops the brochure into the trash.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was right. I had to take care of Ma and do the best I could with what I got. I did like reading, though. I didn’t tell nobody that.

EXT. TONY'S PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

The Narrator chains his bicycle outside the parlor.

SIONNA, late twenties, lean and pale, trips on the uneven pavement and brushes against him. Her shoulder-length brown hair frames extraordinary light-blue eyes. She wears jeans, blue tennis shoes, a black V-neck shirt and a silver fish pendant.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her shirt. They stare at each other.

THE NARRATOR
I work here. I’m just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna. My parents named me after an Irish goddess, and the River Shannon.

THE NARRATOR
You want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want to have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number on a receipt. As she walks away, she looks back over her shoulder.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Like she was the tide coming in, and I didn’t want to move my feet. I wanted her to cover me completely.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Medication bottles stand in a careful line on the kitchen counter. Ma searches among them, frightened.

MA
I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

THE NARRATOR
Ma, we haven’t been to church for fifteen years. Your pills are right here in the box for today.

Ma looks through him with empty, glassy eyes. A beat later, recognition returns.

MA
Oh, right. I am so sorry. I don’t know what got into me.

The Narrator guides her into her chair and covers her with an afghan. Across the room, the community-college brochure protrudes from the trash.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Old lamps hang over endless aisles of books. The Narrator approaches LIBRARIAN MORALES, a composed woman in her fifties.

THE NARRATOR
How do I take a book home from here?

LIBRARIAN MORALES
Do you have a library card?

THE NARRATOR
No. How do I get one of those?

She slides him an application. Soon, he holds a temporary card and wanders into CLASSIC LITERATURE.

His finger passes Shakespeare, Dickens, Dumas and Wilde before selecting WUTHERING HEIGHTS by Emily Brontë.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when you could get everything for free in the library?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Students crowd the fashionable coffee shop. Sionna sits at a rear table with her backpack. The Narrator joins her, clutching Wuthering Heights.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

THE NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA
Yeah. I know. I read it.

She opens the book and sketches connections between ghosts, doubles and the wild moors.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The love Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him. A love that never changes will destroy you.

He thinks of Nick, then of Ma staring at the front door as though his absent father might return.

Sionna slides a fresh community-college application across the table.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside of me was coming awake. She made me feel smarter just by being around her.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

The Narrator lies rigidly on a purple mat among flexible bodies. His shirt falls over his face during downward dog. He hurriedly tucks it into his shorts.

Later, everyone rests in silence. His breath slows. For once, his fists unclench.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

THE NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
You caught on very quickly. You did like it, didn’t you?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah. I did.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

They smile. Beside his rolled mat rests a stack of library books and the completed college application.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Weeks later, Ma sleeps in her chair. A blue weekly pill container sits neatly labeled for morning and night.

Nick turns it over in his huge hands.

NICK
Oh, holy shit. Aren’t you Nurse Nancy? How did you come up with this little idea?

THE NARRATOR
Sionna helped me. She’s smart and good at this shit.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

THE NARRATOR
You haven’t even met her. Give her a break.

NICK
Don’t you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

THE NARRATOR
You’re so Goddamn hateful. What the fuck got into you?

Nick SLAPS him across the head. The Narrator’s ear rings.

NICK
Don’t you ever talk that way about my mother.

Nick slams out. The Narrator remains standing, shocked but unbowed.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Rock music pounds through smoke. Nick spots his former girlfriend LINDSEY beside a pool table with her dark-haired date, ADAM KLINE.

NICK
What’s up, Lindsey? Who’s this?

LINDSEY
Just an old friend.

ADAM KLINE
What the fuck is wrong with you? You got a problem with me?

Nick’s fists close. Lindsey backs against the wall.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, listen. You don’t know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it. You’re going to be next if you don’t get out of my face.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick swings at him. The Narrator recoils as JOE, the burly bar owner, seizes Nick from behind.

Nick head-butts Joe. Patrons scatter. The Narrator bolts through the door.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

The Narrator trembles on Sionna’s worn green sofa. She cups his bruised face.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

THE NARRATOR
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA
He is a controlling ass, and you are showing him that you don’t need him. You are the voice of reason, and he doesn’t understand reason.

THE NARRATOR
I love him. He’s my brother. He always took care of us.

SIONNA
You can’t let him intimidate you or stop you from your purpose.

THE NARRATOR
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you and honor it. To be the best person you can while you’re here.

THE NARRATOR
The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too, honey. Me too.

She kisses his forehead. He closes his eyes in her lap.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - EVENING

Sionna serves Ma warm peach pie. Nick enters and studies Sionna as though she were an opponent.

SIONNA
You must be Nick. Nice to meet you. I’ve heard so much about you.

NICK
Likewise.

SIONNA
You’re just in time for pie.

NICK
No thanks. Sugar makes me feel bloated and sick.

He sits opposite her.

NICK
I hear you’re a university lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
I’m thinking college professor.

NICK
Women can’t stay home and take care of their kids. That’s why this country is heading down the tubes.

SIONNA
Women bring different ways of thinking to the table. Diversity helps us learn. We are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

Nick has no answer. He rises and kisses Ma.

At the door, he pulls the Narrator close by the shirt.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

THE NARRATOR
You know I will.

Over the following weeks, Nick fills Ma’s pill container, brings her food and settles her into the passenger seat of his Trans Am.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The most crazy thing ever was that Nick started taking Ma to Mass. I let him. After all those years, the weight was finally off my shoulders.

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

The burnt-orange Trans Am enters the intersection.

HEADLIGHTS tear in from the side.

Metal CRUSHES metal. Glass bursts across Ma’s still face. The hood eagle folds beneath the wreckage.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The Narrator enters an unnaturally silent apartment. Ma’s purse and coat are gone.

Five missed calls blink on the answering machine.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff’s Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

NURSE PATRICIA OWENS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, this is Patricia Owens at Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

He grabs the telephone with shaking hands.

INT. MILWAUKEE POLICE STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, a broad man with graying hair and a thin mustache, sets down a cup of water.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother and mother were hit broadside last night at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition.

The Narrator waits for the rest.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your mother passed away at the scene.

The cup trembles in the Narrator’s hands. Water spills across the desk.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

A handful of dirt strikes Ma’s light maple casket with a muted THUNK.

Sionna holds the Narrator upright. Nick is absent.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Five days after the crash, Nick lies bruised and yellow beneath white sheets. An oxygen tube enters his nose; another tube drains his punctured lung. His right leg is immobilized.

NICK
Hey, Bro.

THE NARRATOR
Hey.

NICK
I really fucked up, man. I can’t believe Ma’s gone.

Tears slip from Nick’s bloodshot eyes.

THE NARRATOR
The Mass was nice. She’s at peace now.

NICK
I had three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I had to take her. I went anyway. Now they’re talking vehicular manslaughter. Like it was all my fault.

THE NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick’s grief twists instantly into rage.

NICK
You think I meant to kill Ma? You son of a bitch.

The Narrator sees the tubes, the ruined leg and the trapped bully.

He turns and leaves.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Weeks later, Nick sits in a leg brace amid empty containers and legal papers charging him with involuntary manslaughter.

He stares at a photograph of the Narrator and Sionna.

Nick pockets a switchblade and limps out.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Sionna opens her door. Nick pushes inside.

SIONNA
Nick? I never expected you here.

NICK
You have no idea what I’ve been going through.

SIONNA
Why don’t you sit down? We can talk.

NICK
If it wasn’t for you messing around with my brother, everything would be cool. He would be taking care of Ma. She would be alive, and I would be working.

SIONNA
This really has nothing to do with me. If you’ll just listen—

Nick drives her against the hallway wall and clamps one hand around her throat.

Sionna claws at his wrist. Her breath becomes a thin squeak.

Nick flicks open the switchblade.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight. I’ll be back in one week, and you better have cash.

The front door flies open. The Narrator freezes at the sight of them.

THE NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick! What are you doing?

He grabs Nick’s knife arm. Nick jerks away.

The blade plunges into Sionna’s upper left chest near the shoulder.

Blood spreads across her shirt.

Nick elbows the Narrator, wipes the blade on Sionna’s clothes and flees.

The Narrator catches Sionna as she slides down the wall. He tears away the soaked fabric and presses both hands against the wound.

THE NARRATOR
Stay with me. Stay with me.

Her breath grows shallower. Sirens approach.

THE NARRATOR
My brother stabbed her. Send an ambulance!

Paramedics rush inside. A monitor begins BEEPING as they work over her.

The Narrator kneels in her blood.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Months later, Sionna sits in a rocking chair, her repaired shoulder stiff. The light has gone from her blue eyes.

A newspaper on the table reports Nick’s prison sentence for the stabbing and the fatal crash.

THE NARRATOR
Are you depressed? What are you thinking?

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

THE NARRATOR
You’re safe. I’ll protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you. I need to go to Mom and Dad’s for a while.

THE NARRATOR
Don’t do that. Stay here. We can work it out.

She walks into the bedroom and closes the door.

The Narrator stands alone between the bloodstain that will not leave the floorboards and the college books that will not let him stop.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Eight years later, the Narrator is broader, graying at the temples and dressed in the quiet confidence of a successful businessman.

He meditates among candles. His mind drifts past Ma, Sionna and Nick, then settles.

EXT. GREEN HILLS ROAD - DAY

A dream. The Narrator walks through impossibly green hills beside THE SAGE, a dark-skinned Indian man in a flowing white wrap.

A dirty pizza box lies in the perfect road.

THE NARRATOR
How could someone leave that here? It’s an eyesore.

THE SAGE
Why haven’t you learned anything? Stop judging things. Things are neither good nor bad. This is an illusion. Start living the truth.

The Sage and the pizza box vanish.

The Narrator stands alone, smiling with sudden freedom.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

The Narrator wakes beside an orange-and-white cat. Through the window, a sign glows above his first restaurant: TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA.

A regional map carries twelve restaurant pins.

He opens his laptop and books a flight to Milwaukee.

THE NARRATOR
I had to find forgiveness.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTION VISITING ROOM - DAY

Nick enters in prison orange. He is thinner, gray at the sides and diminished by eight years behind bars.

The brothers sit across thick plexiglass and lift yellow telephones.

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

THE NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. I don’t have much time, so I need you to listen.

Nick lowers his eyes.

THE NARRATOR
I’m no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say to me. I’ve spent eight years thinking and growing, and I’ve realized a few things about you.

Nick looks up.

THE NARRATOR
Frankly, you’re an asshole. You killed my mother in a drunk-driving accident. You held me down my entire life so you could have the money and the exciting life. And you tried to kill the love of my life. I’ll never see her again because of you.

Nick’s eyes fill.

THE NARRATOR
Now the important bit. I forgive you.

Nick stares through the glass.

THE NARRATOR
I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Tears roll down Nick’s face and drip onto the counter.

NICK
I’m so sorry. You’re right. I did all that shit. You’re more than I ever was. You’re a big man, and I’m a small one. You deserve to live out there. I deserve to live in here.

A prison officer signals that time is nearly over.

THE NARRATOR
I still believe you can change. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
I love you too. What are you doing now?

THE NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’re expanding. You always said I couldn’t do it. I did it, Nick.

Nick smiles through his tears.

NICK
President Olson. That’s fucking amazing. I’m proud of you, man.

The officer takes Nick by the arm.

NICK
Bye.

The Narrator watches his brother disappear down the corridor.

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT GATE - DAY

An attendant scans a boarding pass bearing the full name PETER OLSON.

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

The Narrator looks at his name and smiles.

THE NARRATOR
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

He walks down the jet bridge without looking back.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

>FADE OUT.

>THE END<
